This not only confirms that at least some dinosaurs had feathers—a theory already widely accepted in scientific circles—but also provides information that fossils generally cannot offer, such as color and three-dimensional arrangement.
Scientists say that this animal's ornamental feathers are brownish oak color on the upper side and carotenoid color on the lower side.
This finding further enhances our understanding of feather evolution in birds.
Since the feathers of this dinosaur lack a well-developed central shaft, known as the rachis, but possess other feather features—barbs and filaments—researchers therefore argue that the evolutionary advancement of the rachis likely occurred later than the development of the other features.
The structure of the feathers indicates that they were not used for flight, but rather for temperature regulation or display. Researchers believe that even if this down belongs to a young dinosaur, the specimen still represents feathers of an adult animal, not the down of a chick.
