In France, voting has traditionally been conducted using basic technology: voters would go into private booths and place a pre-printed ballot made of paper, indicating their chosen candidate, into an envelope.
After the voter identity officials have verified the voter's identity, the voter puts the ballot into the ballot box and signs their name on the voter list.
French election law establishes relatively strict rules for these practices.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can witness that at the beginning of voting no envelope is present inside and that no envelope other than the envelope of eligible voters is added.
Candidates may submit representatives to observe each part of the process. At night, votes are counted by volunteers following specific procedures, under strict supervision.
