When a small group of living organisms (a small community) separates from the main community they belong to (for example, when they relocate to the other side of a mountain range or river, or when they move to a new island from which they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in an environment different from their previous one.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population, in order to become a strong competitor, will need abilities or different adaptations beyond what they previously required.
The main population has not changed in any way; they still need previous adaptations to the environment.
Over time, as the population gradually adapts itself to the new environment, they become less and less similar to other people.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will be so different that they can no longer be considered part of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which means solely the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolution.
