The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the Seven Wonders that still stands today.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the 3rd century BCE, is one of several large pyramidal structures constructed in honor of a deceased pharaoh.
The Giza Plateau, also known as the "Pyramids of Giza," located in the Valley of the Kings in Egypt, includes several pyramids (among which the Great Pyramid is the largest), several small tombs, some temples, and the large statue of Abu Simbel situated within that valley.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of Pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were built in honor of Khufu's wives and family members.
