Technological determinism is a term that actually encompasses a broad spectrum of beliefs, ranging from technological pressure or technological necessity to a precise interpretation of the concept suggesting that a fundamental logic grounded in scientific laws and their emergence in technology governs human destiny.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: that technological development follows a path beyond the influence of culture or politics, and that technology itself exerts greater "impacts" on inherent societies than on conditional societies.
For example, someone might say that the car necessarily caused the development of roads.
But a national highway network is economically unacceptable for a small number of cars; therefore, in order to reduce car ownership costs, new production methods were developed.
Public ownership of vehicles also leads to a high incidence of accidents on roads, which in turn results in the development of new methods in healthcare for repairing damaged bodies.
Romanticism had a significant element of cultural determinism, which originated from writers such as "Goethe," "Fichte," and "Schlegel."
In the text, romanticism shaped individuals, and over time, customs and traditions, along with the culture related to that geography, emerged, and these were in harmony with society's position, being better than the laws that had been arbitrarily imposed.
Just as Paris is known as the capital of modern fashion, Constantinople was regarded as the capital of fashion in feudal Europe.
Its fame began due to its status as a center of aristocracy around the year 400 AD and continued until around the year 1100 AD.
During the twelfth century, its position weakened because the Crusaders had returned their own goods—such as silk and spices, which were worth more than the supplies of the Eastern Roman market.
At the same time, the transfer of the title of capital of fashion from Constantinople to Paris took place.
The Gothic style reached its peak during the period between the 10th–11th and the 14th centuries.
Initially, clothing was mostly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to the slowness of communication channels, styles in the West may be 25 to 30 years behind.
In the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began developing fashion. One of the greatest transformations of that time resulted from the Crusades, and people started using buttons to fasten their clothing.
Subsistence agriculture is agriculture carried out to produce enough food solely to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Subsistence agriculture is a simple and often organic system that uses locally stored native seeds from ecological regions, along with crop rotation or other relatively simple methods, to maximize yield.
Historically, most farmers earned a living through agriculture, and this still exists in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel neglected by societal standards and help them develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures can differ based on the age, ethnicity, social class, place of residence, and/or gender of their members.
The characteristics that distinguish a subculture may be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or a combination of these characteristics.
Members of a subculture often demonstrate their membership through the distinctive and symbolic use of style, which includes fashion, mannerisms, and deliberate dialect.
One of the most common methods used to illustrate the importance of social integration is referring to cases of children who, due to neglect, misfortune, or intentional abuse by adults, failed to learn social integration during their development.
These kinds of children are called "wild." Some of these wild children are kept confined by people (usually their own parents); in some cases, this confinement of the child results from parents' denial of their child's severe mental and physical deficiencies.
Wild children may have experienced severe child abuse or emotional trauma before leaving or running away.
It is claimed that the rest were raised by large animals; it has been said that some lived alone in the wild.
If raised completely by animals other than humans, a wild child exhibits behaviors (within physical limits) almost identical to those of animals, such as fear and indifference toward humans.
Although project-based learning should make learning easier and more engaging, scaffolding goes one step further.
Scaffolding is not a learning method in itself, but rather support provided to individuals who are undergoing a new learning experience, such as using a new computer program or starting a new project.
Scaffolds may be virtual or real; in other words, a teacher is a type of scaffold, but a paper clip in Microsoft Office is also a scaffold.
Virtual scaffolds are embedded in software, and their purpose is inquiry, stimulation, and explaining methods that might be considered challenging for students to carry out independently.
