The stirrup is a support for the rider's foot, hanging from both sides of the saddle.
Although they provide greater stability for the rider, they may pose safety concerns because there is a risk that the rider's legs could get stuck in them.
If a horse rider falls off the horse and his leg gets caught in the stirrup, the horse will be dragged on the ground if it runs away. To minimize this risk, many precautionary measures can be taken.
First of all, most motorcyclists wear motorcycling chukkas, which have a heel and a suede, completely narrow toe.
In the next step, some saddles, especially English saddles, have safety bars that allow the girth leather to detach from the saddle in case the horse rider falls.
